        "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e8{csc}^{2}\u2061t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e8{csc}^{2}\u2061t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8{csc}^{2}\u2061t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int {csc}^{2}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9+\u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
